@page "/guides/time-zones"

<PageTitle>Time zones · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<DocsArticle Class="docs-article--guide">
    <Contents>
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="which-zone" Text="Which zone" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="server-and-webassembly" Text="Server and WebAssembly" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="the-browsers-zone" Text="The browser's zone" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="abbreviations" Text="Abbreviations" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="testing" Text="Testing" />
        <TbTableOfContentsItem TargetId="related" Text="Related" />
    </Contents>
    <Header>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Time zones</TbText>
        <TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
            The dates and times Trailblazor shows follow one zone: a timestamp, a chat message's time, a timeline
            event and a data grid's <code>DateTimeOffset</code> column, in its cells, filter and exports. Set the zone
            once and they all follow it. The date pickers show a value as it is written, with the date and time
            picker's own <code>TimeZone</code> giving a new value its offset.
        </TbText>
    </Header>
    <ChildContent>
        <DocsHeading Id="which-zone" Text="Which zone" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>A date shows in the first of these that is set:</TbText>
        <ol>
            <li>The component's own <code>TimeZone</code>, on a timestamp or a chat.</li>
            <li>A <code>TimeZoneInfo</code> cascaded around it, such as the signed-in user's zone.</li>
            <li><code>options.Times.TimeZone</code> in <code>AddTrailblazor</code>, for an app that shows one zone.</li>
            <li>The <code>TimeProvider</code>'s local zone.</li>
        </ol>
        <CodeBlock Label="Cascading each user's zone" Code="@Cascade" />
        <TbText>An app that shows one zone to everyone sets it once instead.</TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="One zone for everyone" Code="@OneZone" />

        <DocsHeading Id="server-and-webassembly" Text="Server and WebAssembly" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            On WebAssembly the local zone is the browser's, so dates show in the user's zone with nothing set. On Blazor
            Server, and while a page renders on the server, the local zone is the server's, which is rarely the
            user's. Give those apps a zone, from the user's profile or from the browser. A cascaded value doesn't
            cross into an interactive part of a page from a static layout, so cascade it inside the interactive part:
            in <code>Routes.razor</code> when the whole app is interactive, or around each interactive page or
            component otherwise.
        </TbText>

        <DocsHeading Id="the-browsers-zone" Text="The browser's zone" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            <code>IBrowserService.GetTimeZoneAsync()</code> reads the browser's zone, such as America/New_York, once
            the page is interactive. It returns null when .NET doesn't know the zone.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Reading the browser's zone" Code="@Browser" />

        <DocsHeading Id="abbreviations" Text="Abbreviations" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            A timestamp with <code>ShowZone</code> adds its UTC offset, such as UTC−5. .NET has no abbreviations such as
            EST, since they differ by place and some are shared, so name the ones your users know.
        </TbText>
        <CodeBlock Label="Program.cs" Code="@Abbreviations" />

        <DocsHeading Id="testing" Text="Testing" Class="docs-section" />
        <TbText>
            In bUnit, cascade a <code>TimeZoneInfo</code> around the component, or set
            <code>options.Times.TimeZone</code>, so a test reads the same text on every machine. Register a
            <code>FakeTimeProvider</code> as the <code>TimeProvider</code> to fix the current time. The fake browser
            service's <code>TimeZone</code> answers <code>GetTimeZoneAsync()</code>. In Playwright, a context's
            <code>TimezoneId</code> sets the browser's zone, which on Blazor Server changes only what
            <code>GetTimeZoneAsync()</code> returns, not the dates the server renders.
        </TbText>

        <DocsHeading Id="related" Text="Related" Class="docs-section" />
        <ul>
            <li><a href="components/timestamp">Timestamp</a>: a date and time in the app's zone.</li>
            <li>
                <a href="guides/browser">Browser</a>: reading the browser's zone, and the rest of the browser service.
            </li>
            <li><a href="guides/right-to-left">Right-to-left and culture</a>: how dates follow the current culture.</li>
        </ul>
        <GuideFooter Page="GetType()" />
    </ChildContent>
</DocsArticle>

@code {
    private const string Cascade = """
        <CascadingValue Value="_zone">
            <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">...</Router>
        </CascadingValue>

        @code {
            private TimeZoneInfo? _zone;
        }
        """;

    private const string Browser = """
        @inject IBrowserService BrowserService

        @code {
            protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
            {
                if (firstRender)
                {
                    _zone = await BrowserService.GetTimeZoneAsync();
                    StateHasChanged();
                }
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string OneZone = """
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor(options =>
            options.Times.TimeZone = TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById("America/Chicago"));
        """;

    private const string Abbreviations = """
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor(options =>
        {
            options.Times.Abbreviations["America/New_York"] = new("EST", "EDT");
            options.Times.Abbreviations["America/Chicago"] = new("CST", "CDT");
        });
        """;
}
